# Everything That Could Theoretically Be Added

Not a to-do list — a menu. Most of this should NOT be done. Use it to pick
2-3 things deliberately, not to feel behind on the rest.

## Core gameplay mechanics (pick AT MOST 1, beyond what you have)
- Ghost/echo of past self (recommended — cheapest given your engine)
- Evidence/testimony puzzle — contradicting NPC statements to piece together
- Echo-location pulse — brief reveal of hidden paths/objects
- Reputation/trust meter — NPC behavior shifts based on choices
- Light/shadow mechanic — visibility or stealth tied to a light source
- Resource management (battery, oxygen, time) creating urgency
- Dialogue-choice branching with consequences later in the loop
- Environmental storytelling only — no explicit mechanic, just discovery
- Simple rhythm/timing minigame as an occasional set-piece
- Sorting/pattern-matching puzzle (e.g. restoring corrupted data in order)

## World / level content
- More rooms/areas beyond the current city map
- A second, distinct background type (e.g. one corridor-style simpler level)
- Day/night or "glitch intensity" visual variation of the same map
- Secret/optional areas with no mechanical reward, just flavor
- Multiple distinct "chapters" tied to LoopManager.loopCount
- Environmental hazards (not combat — e.g. a closing door, a timer zone)
- More interactive objects beyond the PC (notes, switches, machines)
- NPCs with idle behavior (patrol paths, idle animations, barks)

## Audio
- More SFX: menu click, crack success/fail, footstep, pickup, quest complete
- A second music track (e.g. tension layer that fades in during fights/chases)
- Ambient layer separate from music (hum, wind, distant sound)
- Audio ducking (lower music volume during terminal typing)
- Mute-on-tab-blur (pause audio when browser tab loses focus)

## Visual polish
- Particle effects (dust, sparks, glitch particles) via FxManager
- Screen-space vignette or color grading per "chapter"/theme
- More glitch visual variety (not just shake/flash — chromatic aberration, scanline intensity tied to LoopManager.glitchLevel())
- Weather/ambient animation (rain, flickering lights)
- Death/failure state visual distinct from normal loop reset
- Win-state screen/animation (currently nothing happens on "beating" the game)
- Loading screen / progress bar polish beyond the default
- Custom cursor
- Screen shake variety (directional, not just uniform)

## UI/UX
- Tutorial/onboarding hints (first-time player guidance)
- Keybind remapping UI actually wired to InputRouter (KeybindList element exists — is it used?)
- Accessibility: text size setting actually applied (settings schema has it — is it wired?)
- Toast notifications for more events (already have ToastNotifier — is it used everywhere it could be?)
- Minimap toggle on/off (not everyone wants it always visible)
- Settings: a "controls reference" screen listing all keybinds
- Credits screen
- Quest log: sorting, completed vs active separation
- Inventory: item descriptions/tooltips on hover

## Narrative/content
- NPC dialogue variety (more than one line per NPC)
- Multiple endings based on choices or fragment count
- An optional "lore" collectible track separate from main quests
- Opening cutscene/intro text before gameplay starts
- Epilogue text after completion
- Journal/log screen compiling discovered fragments into readable form

## Companion (cat)
- More animation states (sit, sleep, react to player actions)
- Cat reacts to glitch level (hides, changes behavior as world destabilizes)
- Cat occasionally "finds" something (a light gameplay hook, not required)
- Multiple cat poses already exist (sitting_down_on_back) — is it used anywhere?

## Technical/meta
- Performance pass (check for memory leaks on repeated screen transitions)
- Mobile/touch input support (if relevant for judging on other devices)
- Gamepad support (InputRouter was designed to allow this)
- Save/load persisting progress across browser sessions (localStorage)
- Error handling polish (what happens if an asset genuinely fails to load mid-game?)
- Build size optimization (check dist/ bundle size)

## Submission/presentation (NOT code — do not neglect this)
- itch.io page: screenshots, short description, controls listed clearly
- A short trailer/GIF for the itch.io page
- Clear README or in-game credits
- Test the actual submitted build one final time, fresh download, different browser

---

## How to use this list
1. Pick mechanics from "Core gameplay mechanics" — **zero or one**, not more.
2. Pick 2-3 items total from Visual/Audio/UI combined — whatever gives the
   most "it feels finished" signal for the least time.
3. Everything else stays here, unreached, on purpose. A judge rewards a
   tight, polished, working game far more than a long feature list with
   rough edges.
4. Re-read this list only if you have genuinely spare time in the final
   hours — never let it create anxiety about what's "missing."